<header><a href="/">MAE Academy</a><nav aria-label="Primary"><a href="/simulations">Simulations</a> <a href="/research">Research</a> <a href="/library">Library</a> <a href="/about">About</a> <a href="/contact">Contact</a></nav></header>
<main>
<h1>PLC</h1>
<article lang="en">
<header><h2>Ladder logic on the Siemens Simatic S7-200</h2><p>A programmable logic controller does not run a program once; it repeats a fixed cycle forever. This tool lets you build relay ladder rungs and watch that cycle evaluate them.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and meaning</h3><p>This mode executes a ladder diagram against a model of an S7-200 CPU. You place contacts, coils, timers and counters on rungs, drive the physical inputs <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.0}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.7</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.7}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and watch the outputs <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.0}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.7</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.7}</annotation></semantics></math></span> respond. The same program can be shown as statement list instructions, so each rung is seen both as a diagram and as the boolean stack code the CPU really runs.</p></section>
<section><h3>Mathematical model</h3>
<h4>Memory areas and addressing</h4><p>An address is an area letter, an optional size letter and a byte index, followed by a bit index for single bits. Inputs <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I}</annotation></semantics></math></span> mirror the field terminals and outputs <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></span> drive the load terminals. Variable memory <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">V</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{V}</annotation></semantics></math></span> holds general data, markers <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">M</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{M}</annotation></semantics></math></span> hold internal bits, timers <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">T</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{T}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and counters <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">C</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span> are addressed by instance number such as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">T37</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{T37}</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">C0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{C0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A bit address needs a bit index from <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.7</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.7}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is legal while <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.8</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.8}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is not.</p>
<h4>The scan cycle</h4><p>The CPU repeats four phases: copy the physical inputs into the input image, evaluate every rung in order against that image, latch the output image onto the terminals, then perform housekeeping such as communications. The scan time is the sum <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{scan}</annotation></semantics></math></span> of the four phase durations. Because inputs are frozen during evaluation, a contact reads the same value in every rung of one scan. A change can wait for one full scan before it is read, and its effect is latched within the next, so the worst case response is about <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\,T_{scan}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
<h4>Contacts, coils, set and reset</h4><p>A normally open contact (instructions <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">LD</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LD}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">A</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span>) passes power when its bit is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A normally closed contact (<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">LDN</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{LDN}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">AN</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{AN}</annotation></semantics></math></span>) passes power when the bit is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The coil <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">=</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{=}</annotation></semantics></math></span> copies rung power into its bit every scan. Set <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">S</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{S}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and reset <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">R</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{R}</annotation></semantics></math></span> act only while power flows and then hold their result, and when both act in one scan the later rung wins. The cases are summarised in the equation list below.</p>
<h4>Timers, counters and edges</h4><p>A TON timer counts ticks while its input is true and raises its output once the elapsed value <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ET</annotation></semantics></math></span> reaches the preset <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PT</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The delay is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PT</annotation></semantics></math></span> multiplied by the time base of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; dropping the input clears the timer. A CTU counter increments on each rising edge of its count input, saturates at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>32767</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32767</annotation></semantics></math></span>, clears on reset, and raises its output when the count value <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CV</annotation></semantics></math></span> reaches <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PV</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The edge triggers <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">EU</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{EU}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">ED</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{ED}</annotation></semantics></math></span> pass one scan of power on a rising or falling transition by comparing the present value with the one stored from the previous scan.</p>
<h4>Governing equations</h4>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{scan} = t_{in} + t_{prog} + t_{out} + t_{hk}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Scan time as the sum of input copy, program evaluation, output latch and housekeeping durations.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{resp,\max} \approx 2\,T_{scan}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Worst case input to output response, bounded by two scan times.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.0</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.0</mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.0</mtext><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.1</mtext><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.0} = \left(\texttt{I0.0} + \texttt{Q0.0}\right)\cdot\overline{\texttt{I0.1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Seal-in motor rung: start OR own output, AND NOT stop.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">{</mo><mtable rowspacing="0.36em" columnalign="left left" columnspacing="1em"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mi>Q</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{+} = \begin{cases} 1 &amp; S = 1 \\ 0 &amp; R = 1 \\ Q &amp; S = R = 0 \end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Set and reset coil behaviour with the held state.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi></mrow><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>10</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d} = PT \times \mathrm{Base}, \qquad \mathrm{Base} \in \{1,\ 10,\ 100\}\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>TON delay equals preset times time base, for the three available bases.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">[</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>E</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo fence="true">]</mo></mrow><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>E</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>32767</mn><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{T} = IN \cdot \left[\, ET \ge PT \,\right], \qquad ET^{+} = \min\left(ET + 1,\ 32767\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>TON output and the saturating elapsed value.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>U</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>32767</mn><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">[</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo fence="true">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CV^{+} = \min\left(CV + CU \cdot \overline{CU_{prev}},\ 32767\right), \qquad Q_{C} = \left[\, CV \ge PV \,\right]</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>CTU rising-edge count with saturation and the done condition.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover><mo>⋅</mo><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU = IN \cdot \overline{IN_{prev}}, \qquad ED = \overline{IN} \cdot IN_{prev}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Rising edge EU and falling edge ED detectors.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section><h3>Canvas parameter and control mapping</h3><dl>
<dt>Input and output indicators</dt><dd>The input switches and output lamps display the images of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.0}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.7</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.7}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.0}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.7</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.7}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The hardware profile limits which bytes exist, for example the larger CPU offers more input bytes.</dd>
<dt>Contact variants</dt><dd>Choosing normal open or normal closed selects the polarity <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the rung. The positive and negative edge contacts implement the <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EU</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ED</annotation></semantics></math></span> equations.</dd>
<dt>Output and internal coils</dt><dd>An output coil writes a <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></span> bit and an internal coil writes an <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">M</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{M}</annotation></semantics></math></span> bit, which is invisible to the field but readable by later rungs.</dd>
<dt>Set and reset coils</dt><dd>The count field of a set or reset coil gives the number of consecutive bits <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> starting at the target address that are forced together.</dd>
<dt>Timer block</dt><dd>The time base choice of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the preset operand <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PT</annotation></semantics></math></span> determine <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. TON, TOF and TONR variants are available, and the instance such as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">T37</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{T37}</annotation></semantics></math></span> names the register.</dd>
<dt>Counter block</dt><dd>The preset <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PV</annotation></semantics></math></span> sets the done threshold. The CU pin is driven by rung power and the reset pin by the row beneath, giving the CTU equation.</dd>
<dt>Run control and views</dt><dd>Running the program repeats the scan; the statement list editor and register monitor show the same rungs as stack code and the current values of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CV</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ET</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
</dl></section>
<section><h3>Worked numerical example</h3><h4>Conveyor with start-up delay and batch counter</h4><p>A conveyor uses start <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a stop input <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.1</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and motor <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A TON timer with a time base of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PT = 50</annotation></semantics></math></span> delays a horn. A CTU counter with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PV = 12</annotation></semantics></math></span> counts parts, and the phases of the CPU last <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in} = 1\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{prog} = 5\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out} = 1\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{hk} = 1\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Find the scan time, the worst response, the horn delay and the number of full batches in <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math></span> parts.</p><ol>
<li><p>Add the four phase durations to obtain the scan time.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{scan} = 1 + 5 + 1 + 1 = 8\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>A change just after the input copy waits one scan to be read and is latched in the next.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{resp,\max} \approx 2 \times 8 = 16\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Press start with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.0</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.0} = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">I0.1</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{I0.1} = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then release it. The seal-in keeps the motor on until stop is pressed.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant="monospace">Q0.0</mtext><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Q0.0} = (1 + 0)\cdot 1 = 1, \qquad (0 + 1)\cdot 1 = 1, \qquad (0 + 1)\cdot 0 = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Multiply the preset by the time base for the horn delay.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{d} = 50 \times 100\ \mathrm{ms} = 5000\ \mathrm{ms} = 5\ \mathrm{s}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Each batch needs <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PV = 12</annotation></semantics></math></span> rising edges, so the sixty parts fill whole batches as follows.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{60}{12} = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
</ol><p>The scan lasts <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the response is at most <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\ \mathrm{ms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the horn waits <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\ \mathrm{s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the counter completes <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math></span> batches.</p></section>
<section><h3>Industrial applications and standards</h3><dl>
<dt>Manufacturing lines</dt><dd>Seal-in motor starters, interlocks and batch counters on packaging and assembly conveyors.</dd>
<dt>Robot cells</dt><dd>Cell safety sequencing where guard inputs, a start edge and a part counter release the robot controller.</dd>
<dt>Water and energy utilities</dt><dd>Pump alternation and start-up delays using timers, with set and reset memory for alarm latching.</dd>
<dt>Building automation</dt><dd>Lighting and ventilation staging driven by timers and edge-triggered push buttons.</dd>
</dl></section>
</article>
